 QUESTIONS Q1. Lulu fell on her left knee while playing netball. She experienced severe pain immediately and could not extend the knee. X-ray of the knee revealed no fracture and no dislocation. However the Attending orthopedic surgeon recommended surgery: Mention by use of a phrase of 2 to 5 words what knee apparatus has been injured _____________________________________________________ List the components or levels of possible injury to this apparatus: Q2. This question is about the brachial plexus Joe is a 40 year old watchman employed by the coffee factory in town. One day after night duty he found that his right wrist was weak and he could not extend it. He went to see a doctor. He told the doctor that he had had no injury or remarkable incident. He had slept while sitting on a chair his arm resting and hanging on the back of the chair. In relation to this case write down short answers to these questions: (A) What is the diagnosis?___________________________________________ (B) What important structure has been affected?__________________________ (C) Where is the anatomical position of this structure? ______________________________________________ (D) What is the treatment?___________________________________________","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Orthopedics","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Orthopedics","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f0178dce9997","stem":"Describe the origin, insertion, innervations, and actions of the following muscles.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum","options":[{"label":"A","text":"i) Sartorius"},{"label":"B","text":"ii) Triceps brachii"},{"label":"C","text":"iii) Brachioradialis"},{"label":"D","text":"Discuss joints of the shoulder region under the following headings;"},{"label":"E","text":"i) Name of joints"},{"label":"F","text":"ii) Bones involved forming these joints"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-81036e0794eb","stem":"Discuss joints of the shoulder region under the following headings","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Head","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"i) Name of joints"},{"label":"B","text":"ii) Bones involved forming these joints"},{"label":"C","text":"iii) Range of movements performed by each joint."},{"label":"D","text":"öìöìöìöìöáöáöáöáöáö×öÍ×ÍÂÍÂÍÂÍÂÍáö ̧ö¤ö–öáö ̧ö ̧ö ̧ö¤öŽŠŽŠ"},{"label":"E","text":"÷ó÷óéãéãóéãéØéãóÔóÏóÏóÔÅ"},{"label":"F","text":"°Ð/ °à=!°"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"]},{"id":"q-91cea68bddac","stem":"Describe the origin, insertion, innervations, and actions of the following muscles. i) sartorius ii) Biceps femoris","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum","options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thoracic Wall and Mediastinum · Suggested","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-e568e749f1a6","stem":"TUMAINI UNIVERSITY KILIMANJARO CHRISTIAN MEDICAL UNIVERSITY COLLEGE END OF SEMESTER I EXAMINATIONS (The back, Thorax and Upper limb) CODE : ANA 102 (Completed) SUBJECT : ANATOMY, HISTOLOGY, EMBRYOLOGY AND RADIOLOGY PROGRAMME : MD I DATE : Monday 25th 2013 TIME : 2 HOURS INSTRUCTIONS TO CANDIDATES 1. Make sure that you write your examination Code Number correctly. 2. Read each question carefully. 3. Attempt all questions. Which labeled structure in the photograph of the adult vertebral canal and its related structures develop from the notochord?","type":"Image / Diagram Question","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Thorax","topicOriginal":"Thorax","subtopic":"Thorax","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Thorax","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-c826af0f6b4b","stem":"Immediately after a spontaneous vaginal delivery, a full-term male newborn is found to have a tuft of hair on the midline in the lumbar region. Palpation of the area shows an absence of spinous processes in that region. Imaging studies show no additional abnormalities. The most likely cause of this defect was failure of which of the following events during gestation?","type":"OSCE / Practical Station","subject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"A . Development of the notochord"},{"label":"B","text":"B . Formation of the neural tube"},{"label":"C","text":"E . Migration of the neural crests"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Pediatrics and Child Health","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-a0ab28fb293b","stem":"Identify the pointed structure in figure 3 below: Vertebral spine Lamina Pedicle Vertebral body It is decided to image the spinal cord and spinal nerve rootlets by doing a myelogram (injection of a radio-opaque dye into the subarachnoid space followed by a radiograph). In order to inject the dye without injury to the spinal cord, the injection is usually done below what vertebral level?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Neurology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Neurology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-38d538c341b7","stem":"Dorsal root ganglion Pedicle The myelogram also revealed that the dorsal and ventral rootlets of the 5th and 6th cervical nerves had been avulsed (torn or pulled out) from the spinal cord on the right side. Which nerve fibers would not be damaged by the avulsion?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Neuroanatomy","topicOriginal":"Neuroanatomy","subtopic":"Spinal Cord","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Neuroanatomy","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2a3eb0b16705","stem":"Because of their structure and interconnections, which veins are especially important in the metastatic spread of cancer? Basilic Cephalic Dorsal scapular Internal vertebral venous plexus Transverse cervical Name the structure tagged in the picture below is?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Histology and Tissues","topicOriginal":"Histology and Tissues","subtopic":"Vein","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Histology and Tissues","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-f368efe0c8ab","stem":"Right coronary vein Right coronary artery Left coronary artery Circumflex artery Interventicular artery While moving into a new apartment a student lifting a heavy box of books experiences a sharp pain in his back, radiating down the anterior thigh and medial side of his leg. After several days of misery, he finally goes for treatment and is told that he has a herniated intervertebral disk at the L 4 level which is compressing a spinal nerve where it exits the vertebral column. The point of compression is the: Central canal Foramen magnum Hiatus of the sacral canal Intervertebral foramen Vertebral foramen Which among the following features is tagged in the picture below?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Lower Limb","topicOriginal":"Lower Limb","subtopic":"Thigh","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Lower Limb","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-00bd19f7fb5b","stem":"A 45-year-old man complained to his physician that the muscles of his upper limb were weak and he felt clumsy while walking. Tests revealed that he had amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (Lou Gehrig's disease), a disease which attacks the neurons of the voluntary motor system. Where would one expect to see atrophic or degenerated nerve cell bodies? Dorsal horn of the spinal cord Dorsal root ganglion Lateral horn of the spinal cord Sympathetic chain ganglia Ventral horn of the spinal cord What structure is marked by the red asteric in the picture below? Medial cubital vein Cephalic vein Basilica vein Anterior cubital vein","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7d6c6dc2001b","stem":"In the final stages of labor a caudal anesthetic is sometimes given via a needle inserted into the sacral hiatus. The anesthetic is thus placed around the outside of the sacral spinal nerve roots and into the: Central canal Dural sinus Epidural space Subarachnoid space Subdural space What muscle insert on the area painted blue?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-2f0376f943ed","stem":"During replacement of a dental crown, a 20-year-old man suddenly begins to choke. He recovers, but the dentist requests an x-ray of the chest because part of the crown is missing. Based on the x-ray findings shown, in which of the following structures is the crown most likely to be found?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Radiography and Medical Imaging","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Radiography and Medical Imaging","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-59888b1cbcd6","stem":"Esophagus Right lower lobe bronchus Right main bronchus Right middle lobe bronchus Right upper lobe bronchus A 25-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department 30 minutes after sustaining a stab wound in the second right intercostal space at the midclavicular line. She is having difficulty breathing. Physical examination shows decreased breath sounds on the right side of the chest. A chest x-ray is most likely to show an abnormally increased amount of air in which of the following? Pericardial space Pleural space Atria of the heart Lungs Which of the following represents the primary function of the cell identified by the arrow in the histologic section shown?","type":"Radiological Interpretation","subject":"Histology","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["topic_generic","requires_visual_review"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":true,"displaySubject":"Histology","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["prior_structural_review_flag","requires_visual_review","topic_generic","visual_asset_not_transcribed"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-3a0cdc91d8c7","stem":"Electrical impulse generation Immunoglobulin synthesis Maintenance of vascular tone Oxygen transport Pulmonary surfactant secretion A 25-year-old man has the recent onset of a fever and productive cough. There is a sharp, stabbing pain in the midlateral area of the left side of the chest with inspiration and coughing. Acute pneumonia is diagnosed. Which of the following could be infiltrated with lidocaine to decrease the severity of the pain? Parasympathetic innervation of the bronchioles Parasympathetic innervation of the pericardial sac Somatic innervation of the central diaphragmatic pleura Somatic innervation of the mediastinal pleura An investigator is studying the mechanism of pulmonary edema. He investigates the normal flow of extracellular fluid through the lungs. Which of the following best describes the normal sequential path of extracellular fluid in the lungs? Alveolar capillary","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-231b82c9186b","stem":"of the left hemidiaphragm by a water-density process; the left cardiac border is clearly visible. Which of the following is the most likely location of the pathologic process?","type":"Short Answer","subject":"Human Anatomy","topic":"Head and Neck","topicOriginal":"Head and Neck","subtopic":"Ear","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":true,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Human Anatomy","displayTopic":"Head and Neck","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-74f61a1ab481","stem":"91⁄49ù:6:t:2:ï;-;k;a;è<'<e<¤<ã=\"=a=¡=à> >`> >à?!?a?¢?â@#@d@¦@çA)AjA¬AîB0BrBμB÷C:C}CÀD","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"DGDŠDÎE"},{"label":"B","text":"EUEšEÞF\"FgF«FðG5G{GÀH"},{"label":"C","text":"HKH‘H×I"},{"label":"D","text":"IcI©IðJ7J}JÄK"},{"label":"E","text":"KSKšKâL*LrLoM"},{"label":"F","text":"MJM“MÜN%NnN·O"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Unclassified Health Sciences","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["subject_needs_classification","subject_unclassified","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization","whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-91186968af65","stem":"Following a reaction the enzyme is normally....... at the end of reaction. a) Changed to another enzyme Set free product coenzyme 32. Enzymes and coenzymes both are; reusable food sources proteins products 33. The non polar tails of phospholipids bilayers are homeophobic hydrophobic homeopathic hydrophilic 34. Enzymes are catalysts because they operate to; lower activation energy raise activation energy supply activation energy supply the reactants 35. Two macromolecules, such as proteins, can adhere tightly and specifically to each other. How can weak forces such as electrostatic attraction, van der Waals bonds, hydrogen bonds, and hydrophobic forces lead to such strong adherence? A.","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Lipids","topicOriginal":"Lipids","subtopic":"Lipid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"adherence can be quite strong by having many weak forces involved in molecular adhesion."},{"label":"B","text":"the weak forces can become very strong once non-polar groups are excluded from the inside of the molecules."},{"label":"C","text":"the weak forces are readily converted to covalent bonds, thus leading to strong adherence between molecules."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Lipids","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":[],"cleaningOperations":["whitespace/page_marker_normalization"]},{"id":"q-745bcd821db8","stem":"The building block of DNA are; nucleoside nucleotide c) phosphate bases 37. Which of the following nitrogen base is not a pyrimidine? adenine uracil cytosine thymine 38. Which nitrogen bases make up DNA nucleotide? a) Adenine, thymine, guanine, cytosine b) Adenine, uracil, guanine, cytosine adenine, thymine, uracil, cytosine adenine, thymine, guanine, uracil 39. The following is true about RNA contain thymine instead of uracil contain uracil instead of thymine its molecule is double helix contain different sugar in its strand 40. The structure shown in the diagram is an example of a monomer unit used in the formation of: INCLUDEPICTURE \"http://www.biology.arizona.edu/biochemistry/problem_sets/large_molecules/graphics/06q.gif\" \\* MERGEFORMATINET","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Base","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"RNA B. protein C. DNA D. Polysaccharides 41.The structure shown in the diagram is an example of: INCLUDEPICTURE \"http://www.biology.arizona.edu/biochemistry/problem_sets/large_molecules/graphics/08q.gif\" \\* MERGEFORMATINET"},{"label":"A","text":"a polysaccharide. B. DNA. C. RNA. D. a protein. E. a lipid."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["mcq_missing_options","duplicate_option_labels"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["duplicate_option_labels","mcq_missing_options","nonunique_option_labels","not_enough_options","possible_multiple_questions_in_stem","prior_structural_review_flag","url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1fc204240591","stem":"Non-competitive inhibitors influences the enzyme activity by; a) Competing for the catalytic site with the substrate b) Changing the specificity of the enzyme for the substrate c) Changing the conformation of the enzyme by binding to site other than catalytic site d) Changing the nature of the product formed 43. The following will affect enzyme activity EXCEPT; a) Hydrogen ion concentration b) Concentration of the substrate c) Temperature d) None of the above 44. Enzymes involved in the process of transamination of amino acids are collectively known as; a)alanine transaminase b)aspartate transaminase c)aspartate amino transferance d)amino transferance 45. Always in DNA adenine pair with a) Thymine b) Cytosine c) Guanine d) All of the above 46. Lock-and-key model of enzyme action implies that; a) The substrate change conformation prior to active site interaction b) The active site is complementary in shape to that of the substrate c) The active site of an enzyme always changes as per substrate shape d) The active site is flexible and adjust to substrate 47. A competitive inhibitor of an enzyme has which of the following properties? a) Decreases activation energy b) Interfere with substrate binding to the enzyme c) Causes irreversible inactivation of the enzyme d) It is frequently a feed back inhibitor 48. The following is true about the effect of competitive inhibitors in enzyme kinetics; a) low substrate concentration is needed to achieve the same velocities that were reached in its absence b) Higher substrate concentration is needed to achieve the same velocities that were reached in its absence c) Does not have any effect. d) None of the above 49. The neutral species of amino acids are known as; a) Nucleoside b) Nucleotide c) Zwitterions d) Amides 50.The number of hydrogen bonds between G-C base pairs are; a) 1` b) 2 c) 3 d) 4 51.The following is true about RNA EXCEPT adenine pairs with uracil its backbone is made up of ribose and phosphates guanine pairs with cytocine in its secondary structure none of the above The differences in chemical properties of amino acids is determined by its; amine and carbonyl group alpha carbon side group all of the above The following is true about ATP EXCEPT is made up of contain adenine is made up of ribose sugar is an example of a nucleotide d) is an example of a nucleoside 54. In medium chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency, fatty acids are not completely oxidized to acetyl CoA. Gluconeogenesis during fasting is therefore low, because acetyl CoA is required to activate HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuf2.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/1999/exam4-99/a4-26a-99.html\" \\t \"scene1\"","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuf2.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/1999/exam4-99/a4-26b-99.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"B","text":"glucose 6-phosphatase. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuf2.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/1999/exam4-99/a4-26c-99.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"C","text":"fructose 1,6-bisphosphatase. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuf2.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/1999/exam4-99/a4-26d-99.html\" \\t \"scene1\" pyruvate carboxylase."}],"priority":2,"status":"review","flags":["probable_document_or_multiple_questions"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"review_required","auditFlags":["long_stem_manual_review","prior_structural_review_flag","probable_document_or_multiple_questions","url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-1b54f275424e","stem":"Which of the following enzymes in glycolytic pathway is inhibited by fluoride? a) Pyruvate kinase b) Enolase. c) Aldolase. d) Glucagons 62. Pyruvic acid is a product of: a) Respiration b) TCA c) Glycolysis. d) Glucogenesis 63. During anaerobic glycolysis, there is a net production in the cytosol of lactate and HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-6a-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\"","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"NAD+. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-6b-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"B","text":"NADH. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-6c-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"C","text":"CO2 HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-6d-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"D","text":"ATP."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7abd713f5bfe","stem":"The committed step of the glycolytic pathway is catalyzed by HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-7a-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\"","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"hexokinase/glucokinase. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-7b-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"B","text":"phosphofructokinase-1. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-7c-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"C","text":"aldolase. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-7d-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"D","text":"glycerol 6-phosphate dehydrogenase."}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic","url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-87b7e9e7644f","stem":"In the reaction mechanism for the oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate, which cofactor is required for the decarboxylation step? HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-9a-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\"","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Thiamine pyrophosphate HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-9b-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"B","text":"Lipoic acid HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-9c-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"C","text":"FAD HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-9d-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"D","text":"NAD+"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic","url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-29bf0ef5b360","stem":"In the conversion of 1 molecule of acetyl CoA to carbon dioxide, how many O2 molecules are consumed or produced directly by the reactions of the citric acid cycle? HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-10b-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\"","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"2 produced HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-10c-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"B","text":"1 consumed HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-10d-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"C","text":"2 consumed HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/2000/exam3-00/a3-10e-00.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"D","text":"0 consumed or produced"}],"priority":2,"status":"practice_draft","flags":[],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Medical Biochemistry","displayTopic":"Water, pH and Buffers","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["url_or_navigation_embedded"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-50cc5777b6c4","stem":"Which is an example of a biochemical pathway? a) ATP b) Oxaloacetate c) Glycolysis. d) Catalyst 68. Deamination; a) Can occur in the liver only b) Can occur in the liver, kidneys, and intestinal mucosa. c) Is the end product of protein metabolism d) Is an example of anabolism mechanism in the body 69. The fate of amino acids not required for the synthesis of tissue proteins is; a) Changed to glycogen b) Changed to fats c) Incorporated in glycolysis d) Undergo deamination. 70. The fate of glycerol in the metabolism of lipids include; a) Changed into glucose and glycogen only b) Changed into fats c) Changed into urea d) Changed into glucose, glycogen or energy. 71. The end product of protein metabolism is a) Ammonia b) Ketone bodies c) Urea only d) Urea, fats, and energy. 72. ketone bodies; a) Are naturally toxic in physiological quantities b) Are present in high concentration in plasma c) Are increased when there is an intra-cellular deficiency of glucose. d) Are increased when there is impaired beta oxidation of fats 73. LDLs are the primary plasma carriers of; a) Free fatty acids for delivery to all tissues b) Triacyglycerol derived to the liver c) Glycerol delivery to adipose tissues d) Cholesterol for delivery to all tissues 74. In aerobic glycolysis the main fate of pyruvate is a) Conversion to lactic acid b) Conversion to acetyl-CoA and oxidation via the TCA cycle c) Gluconeogenesis d) Conversion to alcohol 75. The compounds that directly enter one of the five steps of the urea biosynthetic pathway and provide the nitrogen of urea are HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuf2.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/1999/exam4-99/a4-19a-99.html\" \\t \"scene1\" SECTION B. Write T for the correct statements and F for the incorrect statements. (20mrks) 1. Because the six-membered ring structure in Carbohydrates resembles the organic molecule pyran, derivatives with this structure are termed pyranose. Those with five- membered rings resemble the organic molecule furanose and are termed furan. F 2. Epimers is one the Isomers of Carbohydrates that give different orientation of the H and OH groups on carbon number 2, 3 and 5. F 3. Synthesis of DNA from its precursors depends on the Folate Coenzyme. T 4. All the biological catalysts are capable of catalyzing both forward and backward biochemical reactions. F 5. All the Allostelic enzymes do follow the Michelle’s -Mentens equation. F NAD, NADPH and FAD are among the Coenzymes derived from the Niacin and Riboflavin respectively and play an important role in most of the biologic pathways. T Cell to Cell recognition via CHO units in among the general roles of the carbohydrates. T be joined to other macromolecules to form complex CHOs T 10. The most common form of lipid found in food is Triglycerides ...T... 11. Under anaerobic conditions the fate of pyruvate is reduction to lactate. T 12. Also pyruvate can enter the gluconeogenic pathway through the pyruvate carboxylase enzyme to oxaloacetate. T 13. During starvation, however, the brain can derive energy from ketone bodies and acetate which are converted to acetyl-CoA. F 14. Fist step in glycolysis is the ATP-dependent phosphorylation of glucose to glucose 6-phosphate (G6P) and tissue-specific isoenzymes known as hexokinases are used in this step. T 15. Cholesterol synthesized by the liver, as well as any dietary cholesterol in the liver that exceeds hepatic needs, is transported in the serum within HDLs F 16. When the energy level in Erythrocytes is high the pyruvate will enter the glycogenolysis pathway. F 17. Like the regulation of glycolysis occurring at the PFK-1 reaction, the F1, 6BPase reaction is a major point of control of gluconeogenesis. T 18. In adipose tissue insulin increases PDH activity and in cardiac muscle PDH activity is increased by catecholamine.T 19. The cellular requirement for cholesterol as a membrane component is satisfied in one of two ways: either it is synthesized de novo within the cell, or it is supplied from extra-cellular sources, namely, chylomicrons and LDLs.F 20. Life is good when it is understood.T .SECTION. C Short Essay (55mrks) 1. Explain on protein metabolism under the following sub-headings (10marks) -formation of ammonia -excretion of ammonia/urea from the body 2. a) Define ketosis (2marks) b) Briefly discuss on ketosis under the following sub headings (08 marks) - Conditions which may cause the formations of ketone bodies in the body 3. a). Explain as to why there is phosphorylation of glucose and other products in glycolysis (5marks) b) Explain on what you understand by G{RþÒþ\\ù? \"é3⁄4Rýh †z?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Medical Biochemistry","topic":"Water, pH and Buffers","topicOriginal":"Water, pH and Buffers","subtopic":"Acid","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"alanine and glutamine. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuf2.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/1999/exam4-99/a4-19c-99.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"B","text":"aspartate and glutamate. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuf2.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/1999/exam4-99/a4-19d-99.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"C","text":"alanine and ammonium ion. HYPERLINK \"http://bob.usuf2.usuhs.mil/biochem/exams/1999/exam4-99/a4-19e-99.html\" \\t \"scene1\""},{"label":"D","text":"aspartate and ammonium ion. íÜÊÜÊÜ¶ÜÊÜÊÜ¶ÜÊÜ¥Ü¶ÜÊÜ¥Ü—Ü...ÜwfÜT êÙÈÙ¶ÙÈÙ¶Ù ̈—Ù‚p‚p‚p‚p‚ZH ëÚÈÚo©Ú—ÚÈ©Ú†Úo©ÚrÚÈ©ÚÈcÈRÚÈÚÈ©Ú ïÞÌÞoÞoÞoÞoÞoÞoÞoÞ£Þ’ÞoÞ’Þ„ÞsÞsÞsÞsÞoa# íßÓÄ1­1­1›ŠxaŠaŠxŠPŠPŠxŠPE ðåðÖðåÃ«–